{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 2, :comments ["<b>When a person has in the skin of his flesh.</b> This occurs most often when the semen is not refined from menstrual blood." "<b>A swelling or a scab or a bright spot.</b> All of them are types of tzara'at, and their appearance is white, as has been received in tradition. They do not include the types of tzara'at that physicians have enumerated, apart from morphea and albarum and the netek. Indeed, the other severe types of tzara'at that they enumerated — namely cancer of the entire body generally — which tend toward redness and blackness, the Torah does not declare them impure at all. For these four manifestations alone — whose memory for blessing is: se'et and its offspring, baheret and its offspring — come as reproofs for sin, as they said, of blessed memory (Berakhot 5a): whoever has in him one of these four manifestations, it is nothing but an altar of atonement. But the other types of tzara'at that physicians enumerated will not occur among our people at all in the manner of an altar of atonement, unless we reach the extreme of corruption, God forbid, like the other bad diseases of Egypt, or on account of sin in the conduct of food and drink and the like — and there will be no impurity in them at all." "<b>And he shall be brought (to the priest).</b> For anyone who goes to a place to be acted upon is not called בא (coming) then, but is called מובא (brought), as in: תּוּבַל לַמֶּלֶךְ רֵעוֹתֶיהָ, מוּבָאוֹת לָךְ (Psalms 45:15); וְהִגִּישׁוֹ אֲדֹנָיו (Exodus 21:6); וְהֵבִיא הָאִישׁ אֶת אִשְׁתּוֹ (Numbers 5:15). And the opposite: וְנִגְּשׁוּ אֶל הַמִּשְׁפָּט (Deuteronomy 25:1); וְנִקְרַב בַּעַל הַבַּיִת (Exodus 22:7); עַד הָאֱלֹהִים יָבֹא דְּבַר שְׁנֵיהֶם (Exodus 22:8)."], :book :leviticus, :voice :sforno, :lang :en, :chapter 13, :register {:flags {1 ["God"]}}, :notes {2 "Medical terms: מורפיא״ה = morphea (a localized scleroderma producing white patches); אלבארא״ם rendered as albarum (Latin for 'white,' a white form of leprosy); נתק = netek (the bald patch of Leviticus 13:29-37). Talmudic citation normalized to (Berakhot 5a); Sforno gives it as (ברכות ה:)."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T16:36:19.146616926Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}